    meaning: `The tendency to see recognizable patterns, especially faces, in random stimuli.`,
    description: `Pareidolia is a specific form of apophenia—the brain's face-detection system (the fusiform face area) is so sensitive it fires on ambiguous shapes. Seeing faces in clouds, the Virgin Mary in toast, or the man in the moon are classic examples. It is universal across cultures and may have evolutionary advantages: a false positive (seeing a face that isn't there) is less costly than a false negative (missing a predator).`,
