    type: "word",
    phonetic: `/ˌser.ənˈdɪp.ɪ.ti/`,
    partOfSpeech: `noun`,
    meaning: `The occurrence of pleasant or useful discoveries by accident.`,
    description: `Horace Walpole coined serendipity in 1754 from a Persian fairy tale The Three Princes of Serendip (Sri Lanka), whose heroes 'were always making discoveries by accidents and sagacity.' The word captures something important: luck alone isn't serendipity — you must be prepared to recognize the value of what you've stumbled across. Pasteur: 'Chance favors the prepared mind.'`,
