    phonetic: `/ˌɒnˈwiː/`,
    partOfSpeech: `noun`,
    meaning: `A feeling of listlessness and dissatisfaction arising from a lack of occupation or excitement.`,
    description: `Ennui is more than boredom — it is an existential weariness, a dissatisfaction that cannot name its object. The Romantics saw it as the affliction of the overly sensitive; Kierkegaard saw it as the root of the demonic; Camus saw it as the beginning of awareness. It is the discomfort that precedes the question 'what is this all for?'`,
    examples: [`The resort was beautiful, but an inexplicable ennui set in by the third day.`, `Ennui drove him to read everything — anything to fill the hollow.`, `Camus: ennui is the moment when the stage set collapses and we see the world as it is.`],
