    phonetic: `/ˈæn.ɪk.dəʊt/`,
    partOfSpeech: `noun`,
    meaning: `A short, amusing or interesting account of a real incident or person.`,
    description: `The anecdote is one of rhetoric's oldest tools — concrete, memorable, and emotionally resonant where abstraction fails. But 'anecdote is not data' is equally important: single stories can be deeply misleading. The plural of anecdote is not data. Good thinkers use anecdotes to illustrate, not to prove. Understanding this distinction separates narrative reasoning from systematic evidence.`,
