    description: `Sui generis describes something so distinctive it cannot be placed in any existing category — it is its own category. Used in law for cases that don't fit standard classifications, in art for works that defy genre, and in philosophy for entities that require their own analysis. Being called sui generis is one of the higher compliments in intellectual discourse.`,
    examples: [`Her voice was sui generis — influenced by jazz, gospel, and Lebanese folk music in equal measure.`, `The case was legally sui generis — no precedent applied to its particular configuration.`, `Joyce's Ulysses is sui generis; it established a genre that only it fully occupies.`],
