    phonetic: `/ˌreɪ.ɪ.fɪˈkeɪ.ʃ(ə)n/`,
    partOfSpeech: `noun`,
    meaning: `The process of treating an abstraction as if it were a concrete thing.`,
    description: `Reification mistakes the map for the territory. When GDP becomes 'the economy' rather than one measure of it, reification has occurred. When a model of human behavior is treated as human behavior, reification has occurred. Marxist theory used it for the process by which social relations come to appear as relations between things. Recognizing reification is a form of intellectual hygiene.`,
