    type: "phrase",
    phonetic: `/ˌzɪər.əʊ ˈsʌm/`,
    partOfSpeech: `analytical phrase`,
    meaning: `A situation in which one party's gain is exactly balanced by another's loss.`,
    description: `From game theory: in a zero-sum game, total payoff is constant — one player's gain is another's loss. Chess is zero-sum. Most business, however, is not: two companies can both grow. Mistakenly treating positive-sum situations as zero-sum leads to unnecessary conflict and destroyed value. Much of geopolitics and business strategy involves diagnosing which situation you are actually in.`,
    examples: [`Salary negotiation feels zero-sum but rarely is — creative solutions can expand the pie.`, `The trade war rhetoric was zero-sum thinking applied to what is fundamentally a positive-sum relationship.`, `Recognizing that the relationship was not zero-sum changed everything — both parties could win.`],
