    phonetic: `/ɪˈnɜː.ʃ.ə/`,
    partOfSpeech: `noun`,
    meaning: `The tendency of an object (or system) to resist changes to its state; reluctance to change.`,
    description: `Newton's first law: an object at rest stays at rest; an object in motion stays in motion. The metaphor extends everywhere — institutional inertia, habit inertia, emotional inertia. The insight is that change requires a force to overcome existing states. Understanding inertia helps explain why good ideas fail to spread, why bad practices persist, and why starting is often harder than continuing.`,
